<resources>
    <!-- Android 12+ system splash: a bare coloured field, and deliberately nothing on it.

         🔴 The mark USED to be here, as windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon, so the cold start
         looked like the launcher icon opening. It cannot be, now that GridlinkIntroOverlay
         exists: the splash held the FINISHED mark for as long as the app took to draw, and
         then the intro took it apart and built it again. Measured on the emulator the splash
         was still up 2.3s in, so what you actually saw was the logo, assembled and sitting
         still, and then that same logo scattering into nine blocks and reassembling. The
         animation only means anything if the mark has not already been shown finished.

         🔴 And deleting the attribute is NOT how you get rid of it. With no icon named, the
         platform falls back to android:icon from the manifest, which is the same mark — the
         double-logo survived the deletion unchanged and had to be caught on a device a second
         time. A transparent drawable is the only way to say "no icon" to this API.

         ⚠️ Devices below API 31 never had one anyway: values/themes.xml sets a window
         background and no icon. The branding is the intro's job on every version now. Launches
         that skip the intro (a notification tap, a mailto: link; see MainActivity.playIntro)
         get the bare field and go straight where they were asked to go, which is the right
         trade: those users asked for a message, not for a logo.

         Uses the platform attributes directly (no androidx.core:core-splashscreen needed). -->
    <style name="Theme.Gridlink.Splash" parent="Theme.Gridlink">
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/gridlink_splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/gridlink_splash_blank</item>
    </style>
</resources>
